WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.002
This practice asks you to predict the population before running the query.

00:00:05.002 --> 00:00:08.682
Choice A places the Glucose condition inside ON.

00:00:08.682 --> 00:00:13.852
Because patients is on the left side of the LEFT JOIN, every patient remains.

00:00:13.852 --> 00:00:18.077
The condition only controls which lab rows are eligible to match.

00:00:18.077 --> 00:00:21.567
Choice B places the Glucose condition in WHERE.

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:29.822
The LEFT JOIN may initially preserve every patient, but the final WHERE filter removes rows where the lab

00:00:29.822 --> 00:00:34.037
test is not Glucose, including the NULL lab values for patients with no Glucose match.

00:00:34.037 --> 00:00:37.052
Before you press Run, state your prediction.

00:00:37.052 --> 00:00:39.777
Choice A should return every patient.

00:00:39.777 --> 00:00:45.257
Some rows will contain Glucose information and some will contain NULL lab values.

00:00:45.257 --> 00:00:48.702
Then run Choice A and verify the result.

00:00:48.702 --> 00:00:54.592
The point of this exercise is to build a habit of predicting semantics before execution.

00:00:54.592 --> 00:01:01.387
SQL becomes much easier to debug when you already know what kind of population and grain you expect.
